    "text": "**Case A**\n\n- **A1: Cannot tell.** The search covered only the past seven days. Older misuse, if any, would not appear. It is also not established that the burst was misuse rather than an unusual legitimate change.\n- **A2: Cannot tell.** The burst happened before the check ran at 30 minutes. But we don't know what data the check could see, and we don't know the burst was a compromise.\n- **A3: No, not by itself.** A shared payout destination is a link between the accounts. It could also come from a shared processor, a payroll service or fraud, so it does not prove one owner.\n- **A4: No.** If the payouts went to an attacker, there may be no victim who notices and reports. Thirty days without reports or an incident finding mostly means nobody has looked yet.\n- **A5: Yes.** Three changes came together within 19 minutes of publication: a new proxy and fingerprint, a webhook plus a new payout destination, and a faster payout schedule. That is a classic takeover pattern.\n\n**Case B**\n\n- **B1: Yes.** D has 0 chargebacks out of 100 transactions. A, B and C each have 2 out of 100.\n- **B2: No.** A combined rate of 5 percent is an average. One account can be above it if others are below.\n- **B3: Yes.** The search covered all merchant accounts and found only A and the closed account. So the person does not appear on B, C or D, or anywhere else.\n- **B4: Cannot tell.** The notes were written by different analysts on different days. They may still rely on the same risk-model output or evidence, so we can't confirm they are independent.\n- **B5: No.** A payment blocked as fraud never completed, so no chargeback could arise from it. Having no chargeback says nothing about whether it was legitimate.\n- **B6: Yes.** Shared templates and a shared descriptor pattern across storefronts settling through all four accounts justify investigating them together. They do not prove one operation.\n\nRESULT CO-048 | A1: Cannot tell | A2: Cannot tell | A3: No, not by itself